### What is the Longest Valid Parentheses problem?

Given a string containing only `(` and `)`, find the length of the longest contiguous substring that forms a valid parentheses sequence. For example, in `"(()"` the longest valid substring is `"()"` with length 2. In `")()())"` the longest is `"()()"` with length 4.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class LongestValidParentheses {
    public static int longestValid(String s) {
        Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        stack.push(-1); // sentinel
        int maxLen = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            if (s.charAt(i) == '(') {
                stack.push(i);
            } else {
                stack.pop();
                if (stack.isEmpty()) {
                    stack.push(i); // new base
                } else {
                    maxLen = Math.max(maxLen, i - stack.peek());
                }
            }
        }
        return maxLen;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(longestValid("(()")); // 2
        System.out.println(longestValid(")()())")); // 4
        System.out.println(longestValid("")); // 0
    }
}
```

```text
2
4
0
```

### How does the stack-of-indices approach work?

The stack holds indices, not characters. You seed it with -1 as a sentinel representing the position just before a valid run. For each `(`, push its index. For each `)`, pop the top.

After popping, if the stack is empty, this `)` has no match, so push its index as a new base. If the stack is not empty, the new top is the position just before the current valid run, so the valid length is `i - stack.peek()`. Track the maximum.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class StackTraceDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String s = ")()())";
        Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        stack.push(-1);
        int maxLen = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            if (s.charAt(i) == '(') {
                stack.push(i);
            } else {
                stack.pop();
                if (stack.isEmpty()) {
                    stack.push(i);
                    System.out.println("i=" + i + ": new base");
                } else {
                    int len = i - stack.peek();
                    maxLen = Math.max(maxLen, len);
                    System.out.println("i=" + i + ": valid len=" + len + ", max=" + maxLen);
                }
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Longest: " + maxLen);
    }
}
```

```text
i=0: new base
i=2: valid len=2, max=2
i=4: valid len=4, max=4
i=5: new base
Longest: 4
```

### Why is the sentinel -1 important?

The sentinel lets the very first valid run be measured without special casing. A run ending at index i that started at the beginning has length `i - (-1) = i + 1`. Without the sentinel, you would need extra logic to handle runs starting at index 0.

### What is the constant-space two-pass alternative?

Scan left to right, counting opens and closes. When they are equal, record the current length (2 * opens). When closes exceed opens, reset both to zero. Then do the same scan right to left (resetting when opens exceed closes). Take the maximum from both scans.

The first scan misses valid sequences where opens always exceed closes (like `"(()"` never balances going left to right). The reverse scan catches those. Together they cover all cases.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class TwoPassSolution {
    public static int longestValid(String s) {
        int maxLen = 0, open = 0, close = 0;

        // Left to right
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            if (s.charAt(i) == '(') open++; else close++;
            if (open == close) maxLen = Math.max(maxLen, 2 * open);
            else if (close > open) { open = 0; close = 0; }
        }

        open = 0; close = 0;
        // Right to left
        for (int i = s.length() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            if (s.charAt(i) == '(') open++; else close++;
            if (open == close) maxLen = Math.max(maxLen, 2 * open);
            else if (open > close) { open = 0; close = 0; }
        }

        return maxLen;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(longestValid("(()")); // 2
        System.out.println(longestValid(")()())")); // 4
    }
}
```

```text
2
4
```

### What is the DP approach?

A 1D DP where `dp[i]` is the length of the longest valid parentheses ending at index i. If `s[i]` is `(`, `dp[i]` is 0 (a valid sequence cannot end with an open). If `s[i]` is `)` and `s[i-1]` is `(`, then `dp[i] = dp[i-2] + 2`. If `s[i]` is `)` and `s[i-1]` is `)`, check the character just before the valid sequence ending at i-1.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class DPApproach {
    public static int longestValid(String s) {
        int n = s.length();
        int[] dp = new int[n];
        int maxLen = 0;

        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            if (s.charAt(i) == ')') {
                if (s.charAt(i - 1) == '(') {
                    dp[i] = (i >= 2 ? dp[i - 2] : 0) + 2;
                } else if (i - dp[i - 1] - 1 >= 0 && s.charAt(i - dp[i - 1] - 1) == '(') {
                    dp[i] = dp[i - 1] + 2 + (i - dp[i - 1] - 2 >= 0 ? dp[i - dp[i - 1] - 2] : 0);
                }
                maxLen = Math.max(maxLen, dp[i]);
            }
        }
        return maxLen;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(longestValid("()(()")); // 2
        System.out.println(longestValid("()(())")); // 6
    }
}
```

```text
2
6
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include empty strings, all open, all close, single characters, and strings where the entire string is valid.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class ValidParensEdgeCases {
    public static int longestValid(String s) {
        Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        stack.push(-1);
        int max = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            if (s.charAt(i) == '(') stack.push(i);
            else { stack.pop(); if (stack.isEmpty()) stack.push(i); else max = Math.max(max, i - stack.peek()); }
        }
        return max;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Empty: " + longestValid(""));         // 0
        System.out.println("All open: " + longestValid("((("));   // 0
        System.out.println("All close: " + longestValid("))"));   // 0
        System.out.println("Full valid: " + longestValid("()()")); // 4
    }
}
```

```text
Empty: 0
All open: 0
All close: 0
Full valid: 4
```

### What is the banking use case?

At National Bank of India, Pankaj's protocol parser finds the longest well-framed stretch of a message where every open marker has a matching close, to isolate the largest intact segment after corruption. The stack-based approach identifies the longest contiguous valid portion, and the two-pass counting alternative is used on memory-constrained infrastructure.

### How do the three approaches compare?

| Approach | Time | Space | Clarity |
|---|---|---|---|
| Stack of indices | O(n) | O(n) | Most intuitive |
| Two-pass counting | O(n) | O(1) | Best space |
| DP array | O(n) | O(n) | Most complex |
